    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d426-a1">

                <pb facs="0616" n="492" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg492"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Dean of the Diplomatic Corps</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Pastor</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the American Minister</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Crane</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-12-03"><hi rendition="#italic">December 3,
                        1920</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">Circular No. 239</seg></opener>
                <p>The Dean has the honour to circulate herewith to his Honourable Colleagues
                    translation of a Note dated the 2nd instant, which he has received from the Wai
                    Chiao Pu, proposing that the usual <pb facs="0617" n="493" xml:id="pg493"/>
                    deduction of 13.7% from surplus Customs Revenue for the (eventual) use of the
                    South-Western Provinces should no longer be made.</p>
                <p>Don Luis Pastor<anchor n="85" xml:id="d426-f1"/> is of the opinion that, until
                    unification of North and South is nearer realisation, it would be premature to
                    consent-to this proposal.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">[Translation<anchor n="86" xml:id="d426-f2"/>]</p>
                <p>In agreement with His Excellency the Dean</p>
                <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Maugras</hi><anchor n="87"
                        xml:id="d426-f3"/></p>
                <p>I also agree</p>
                <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rodrigues Alves</hi><anchor n="88"
                        xml:id="d426-f4"/></p>
                <p>It seems to follow from the letter of the Wai Chiao Pu that the Ministry of
                    Finance has the intention of giving, or has already given, to the Inspector
                    General of Customs the instructions in question without the previous assent of
                    the diplomatic corps. I am of the opinion that occasion should be taken in the
                    reply which will be given to this letter to call the attention of the Wai Chiao
                    Pu to this fact and to reiterate the principle that the Inspector General may
                    not receive instructions on the subject of the use of the Customs Revenue before
                    an agreement to that effect has been reached between the Chinese Government and
                    the diplomatic corps.</p>
                <p>December 4, 1920.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Everts</hi></signed><anchor
                        n="89" xml:id="d426-f5"/></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
